1060:  {256((1-2v)^2-3T)^3}.
1061: \]
1062: All units in this section are holomorphic and nonvanishing on a
1063: smaller two-node polydisc; their logarithms are fixed there.
1064: 
1065: Here is the marked section map, including its lift rather than just
1066: its class in the Tate group. The convergent Tate Weierstrass sums
1067: extend over the node chart with coordinates \(Z_0,W_0\) and
1068: \(Q=Z_0W_0\). In these coordinates their first terms are
1069: \(x_T=Z_0+W_0+\text{higher terms}\) and
1070: \(y_T=-W_0+\text{higher terms}\). They consequently give the
1071: invertible analytic node map to the Weierstrass node, after the
1072: chosen \(c_4\)-root scaling and the Weierstrass translation. This is
1073: also seen directly by writing each negative-index summand as a
1074: power series in \(W_0\) and \(Q\); the positive-index summands are
1075: power series in \(Z_0\) and \(Q\).
1076: 
1077: The actual section \(G\) lifts through this node chart. Its two
1078: coordinates vanish on opposite discriminant branches. Choose the
1079: branch which, on the selected \(q_1=0\) normalization, represents
1080: the lift \(Q P_G\). The linear terms of the explicit Morse map in
1081: Section 10 are \(v+T/2\) and \(v-T/2\); hence those two vanishing
1082: orders are one. Analytic division gives
1083: \[
1084:  Z_0(G)=q_1u_G,\qquad W_0(G)=q_2u'_G,\qquad
1085:  P=P_G=\frac{Z_0(G)}Q=q_2^{-1}u_P.                   \tag{SZ.40}
1086: \]
1087: The branch is checked against the exact selected nodal value
1088: \((1+\sqrt{1+v})/(1-\sqrt{1+v})\). Since
1089: \(q_2|_{q_1=0}=-4v+O(v^2)\), it gives \(u_P(0)=16\).
1090: Thus (SZ.40) retains the actual section and the marked period
1091: lift, including its integral reindexing.
1092: 
1093: The line and its primitive translation have an equally direct
1094: formula. Set
1095: \[
1096:  f_P(Z)=\frac{\Theta(Z/P)}{\Theta(Z)}.
1097: \]
1098: The exact divisor and multiplier identify \(f_P\) with the
1099: canonical meromorphic section of the actual Cartier line. The
1100: function \(X\), whose divisor is \(G+(-G)-2O\), consequently has
1101: the literal expression
1102: \[
1103:  X(Z)=c_X f_P(Z)f_{P^{-1}}(Z).                       \tag{SZ.41}
1104: \]
1105: To see its scalar at the collision, evaluate at a fixed ordinary
1106: Tate point, for example \(Z=-1\). The identity
1107: \(\Theta(QZP)=-(ZP)^{-1}\Theta(ZP)\) shows that
1108: \(q_2 f_P(-1)f_{P^{-1}}(-1)\) is a nonzero holomorphic
1109: unit. The actual value \(X(-1)\) is a holomorphic unit there.
1110: Hence
1111: \[
1112:                          c_X=q_2u_X,\qquad u_X(0)=-1/4 .
1113:                                                                \tag{SZ.42}
1114: \]
1115: For the last value, the limiting source is
1116: \(Y^2=X^3+X^2\), so the retained scaling is
1117: \(X=4Z/(1-Z)^2\), and (SZ.41) gives \(c_X\sim-4/P\).
1118: No rational frame is discarded in this evaluation.
1119: 
1120: The target-point primitive lift of Section 6 is now exactly
1121: \[
1122:  U_a:(Z,V)\longmapsto(P^{-1}Z,c_X^{-a}V).             \tag{SZ.43}
1123: \]
1124: Indeed
1125: \[
1126:  f_P(Z)f_{P^{-1}}(Z/P)=1,
1127: \]
1128: so substituting (SZ.41) into the source and target Cartier
1129: coefficients cancels every \(Z\)-dependent factor. For \(a=2\)
1130: the literal map (SZ.16) is applied first. The resulting identity
1131: retains its central Cartier component.
1132: 
1133: Combining (SZ.18) and (SZ.43), the two actual periods are
1134: \[
1135:  \begin{split}
1136:  h:(Z,V)&\longmapsto
1137:        (QZ,P^aV),\\
1138:  g:(Z,V)&\longmapsto
1139:        (P^{-b}Z,\,
1140:         2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0v^Nq_1c_X^{-2}V).
1141:  \end{split}                                                   \tag{SZ.44}
1142: \]
1143: Their valuation matrices along \(q_1=0,q_2=0,v=0\) are precisely
1144: \[
1145:  I,\qquad W=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix},
1146:  \qquad E=NE_{22}.
1147: \]
1148: Every remaining factor in (SZ.44) extends as a unit. This proves
1149: the actual period/unit input to the sector fan construction; the
1150: earlier winding calculation alone was not used as that input.
1151: 
1152: ## 20. Residual periods on the two rank-one faces
1153: 
1154: The rank-one face actions retain nontrivial units. At the
1155: \(q_2=0\) face use the integral target character
1156: \[
1157:                          Y=Z^aV
1158: \]
1159: and the period basis \(h,\ k=gh^{-b}\). The kernel of \(W\)
1160: is generated by \((-b,1)\), and (SZ.44) gives exactly
1161: \[
1162:  \begin{split}
1163:   k:Z&\longmapsto (q_1u_Qu_P)^{-b}Z,\\
1164:   k:Y&\longmapsto
1165:    \frac{2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0v^N}
1166:         {q_1u_Q^2u_P^4u_X^2}\,Y.                    \tag{SZ.45}
1167:  \end{split}
1168: \]
1169: The other period has source multiplier \(Q\) and line multiplier
1170: \[
1171:                          c=(QP)^a=(q_1u_Qu_P)^a .
1172: \]
1173: Thus its actual central source is the nodal quotient of the Tate
1174: chain, with the degree-zero line glued by \(c\). The kernel map
1175: (SZ.45) acts on that line, including the source automorphism
1176: on the normalization. It has not been replaced by the identity.
1177: 
1178: One can verify a uniform contraction on this retained line near
1179: the strict \(q_2\) face. Restrict to
1180: \(|q_2|\leq\eta|q_1|\), with fixed sufficiently small \(\eta\).
1181: Then \(|v|\) is comparable with \(|q_1|\).
1182: Use the usual Tate fundamental annulus
1183: \(|Q|\leq|Z|\leq1\), and interpolate a positive line metric
1184: between its two end values, whose ratio is \(|c|\).
1185: Its maximum-to-minimum ratio is at most
1186: \(C|q_1|^{-a}\). The same interpolation on the plumbing annuli
1187: extends to the nodal normalization, with its prescribed gluing
1188: at the two preimages of the node.
1189: 
1190: The source multiplier in (SZ.45) has magnitude bounded by
1191: \(C|q_1|^{-b}\), whereas \(|Q|\leq C|q_1|^2\).
1192: For sufficiently small \(|q_1|\), reindexing its image into the
1193: fundamental annulus uses at most two source periods when
1194: \((a,b)=(1,2)\), and at most one when \((a,b)=(2,1)\).
1195: Each reindexing contributes at most \(C|q_1|^{-a}\)
1196: to the line norm ratio. Formula (SZ.45) consequently gives the
1197: operator bounds
1198: \[
1199:  \|k\|\leq
1200:  \begin{cases}
1201:  C|\lambda_0|\,|q_1|^{N-4},&(a,b)=(1,2),\\
1202:  C|\lambda_0|\,|q_1|^{N-5},&(a,b)=(2,1).
1203:  \end{cases}                                                   \tag{SZ.46}
1204: \]
1205: For \(N\ge6\) these are strict contractions after shrinking.
1206: On compact subsets away from the exceptional intersection,
1207: the same conclusion follows by decreasing \(|\lambda_0|\).
1208: The norm shell therefore proves freeness and proper
1209: discontinuity of the retained kernel period on its nonzero
1210: line. At \(q_2=0\) this is a statement on the actual normalized
1211: nodal line with its node gluing, not on a substitute product.
1212: 
1213: At the strict \(v=0\) face the kernel of \(E\) is generated by
1214: the source period \(h\). Its transverse character is \(Z\),
1215: and its exact multiplier is \(Q=q_1q_2u_Q\). On that face,
